***Theorem : An algorithm that can plan the motion (i.e., finds the control sequence) for a String-Cluster system can be applied to plan the motion for any (ESATC) system of stress-engineered microrobots.***

***Proof:*** A consequence of Lemma 2; a ***string-cluster*** control matrix can be constructed for any *M-****(ESATC)*** microrobotic system.

Theorem 1 allows us to further reduce the control bandwidth requirements𝜉𝑛. The control voltage bandwidth requirement for a microrobot system with 𝐾 independent snap-down voltage levels and 𝐿 *independent* release voltage levels is 𝜉𝑛 = 𝐾+ 𝐿. In an ***Electromechanically SATurated Cluster system (ESATC),*** the number of non-degenerate microrobots, is 𝑛 = (𝐾 − 1)( 𝐿) or 𝑛 = (𝐾)( 𝐿 − 1). It follows that *n* is maximized when 𝐿 = 𝐾 = 𝜉𝑛 */*2, and 𝑛 = ⌈1 + √1 + 4𝑛 ⌉ ≈ ⌈1 + √4𝑛 ⌉ = ⌈1 + 2√𝑛 ⌉. We call such a system *symmetric* ***Electromechanically SATurated Cluster system (SESATC)***. Table. 1 compares the control voltage bandwidth requirements, the number of control pulses in the control cycle, Reconfigurability and Multi-shapes-Assembly of the five classes of microrobotic systems: a) *NHG*, b) *STRING*, c) *SESat*, d) *String-Cluster* and e) *SESATC*.

